  "description": "When changing local user passwords at first boot, the password update may not be preserved unless the configuration is explicitly saved. This will leave the system in a state where it still accepts the old password, and the new password cannot be used to log in.\n\nThis vulnerability occurs when all of the following conditions are met:\n\nYou have upgraded your BIG-IP system to a version listed in the Versions known to be vulnerable column in the",
  "epss_score": null,
  "f5_bug_id": "536751",
  "has_eol_affected": false,
  "id": "K37250780",
  "impact": "Users cannot log in to the BIG-IP, BIG-IQ, F5 iWorkflow, or Enterprise Manager system using the most recent password, due to the new password being overwritten by the previous password. Additionally, local users are prompted to change their passwords, multiple times, due to password expiration.",
  "is_exposure": true,
  "is_out_of_band": true,
  "kev": false,
  "kev_date_added": null,
  "kev_ransomware": null,
  "mitigation": null,
  "priority": null,
  "priority_score": null,
  "published_date": "2015-12-30",
  "recommended_actions": "If you are running a version listed in the Versions known to be vulnerable column, you can eliminate this vulnerability by upgrading to a version listed in the Versions known to be not vulnerable column. If the table lists only an older version than what you are currently running, or does not list a non-vulnerable version, then no upgrade candidate currently exists.\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you can save the changed password immediately. To do so, perform the following procedure:\n\nImpact of action: Performing the following procedure should not have a negative impact on your system.\n\nLog in to the TMOS Shell (tmsh) of the affected BIG-IP system by entering the following command:\n\ntmsh\n\nChange the user password by using the following command syntax:\n\nmodify auth user <user name> password <password>\n\nFor example, to change the password for a user named exampleuser, to examplepw, enter the following command:\n\nmodify auth user exampleuser password examplepw\n\nSave the configuration by entering the following command:\n\nsave sys config",
